sensibility <b>if </b>PHP <b>doesn't work</b> correctly.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If you are having technical hitches with PHP, I'll be glad to help you.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "They are never read by the program, so if you run the program, it will never be seen.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If you are having trouble with that, you will have it with the case sensitivity, too.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Passive of Modal Verbs: Can", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use the <strong>passive voice</strong> when we want to emphasize in the object and not in the subject that does it. Passive voice can be used anytime; we are going to focus on <strong>can</strong> and how to use it in questions.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>Can</strong> is used to express possibility, permission or ability. In this case, you use <b> can </b> in questions to ask for an object's function. <br><br> To use <b> can</b> in yes/no questions, we use the following structure:<br/><br/> <center> <b>Can</b> + object+ <b>be</b> + <b> verb in past participle</b> + complement? </center></b> <br>Examples: <i><br><b>Can</b> this document <b>be modified </b>by using PHP?<br> <b>Can</b> a line <b>be excecuted</b> in PHP? </i></span><br/><br/>  In case, you want to use <b> can </b>with wh-questions, follow this structure: <center><br> Wh-word ( what, where, when...) + <b>can</b> + object + <b>be</b> + <b>verb in past participle </b> + complement? </center> <br><br>Examples:<br/>   <span class=\"italic\">Where <b>can</b> PHP syntax <b>be used</b>?<br/>How <b>can </b> a PHP code <b>be modified</b>?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Can PHP scripts be placed anywhere in the document?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "How can a line be written but not read? What is its purpose then?", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "remind ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to help someone remember something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Programmers put it to remind themselves of anything they did.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "technical hitch", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a problem related to the use of technology ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: If you are having technical hitches with PHP, I'll be glad to help you.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "suggestion ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a piece of advice or idea you give to another person ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Other developers can see their ideas, comments, or even suggestions about the script. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "figure out ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to realize, to understand something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You could figure out PHP codes if you ask the teacher to explain to you.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "extension ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the letters that a file has at the end of the name; they go after a period", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: About the extension, remember that all the PHP files use \u201c.php\u201d as an extension; the scripts start with \u201c< ?php\u201d and end with \u201c? > :\u201d .", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "get tangled ", "type": "name"}, {"c